course: dictionary
lesson: 6760
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word opening has several meanings. Look at the words around it to find the right one. «The opening of the boating season» is about the day something starts. «An opening in the crust» is a gap that something can pass through.
explain: Test your choice by putting your meaning into the sentence. «A volcano is an opening in the crust where melted rock escapes» still makes sense with gap or hole, so that is the meaning here.
explain: Opening can also be a verb form or an adjective. Open is the verb: to open a door. Opening is its -ing form, so it can describe a noun, as in «the opening scene», which is the first scene.
fact: An opening is a gap or hole that something can pass through.
fact: An opening can be the start of a story, film, game or match.
fact: An opening can be a chance, such as a job opening.
fact: «Opening hours» are the times when a shop or place is open.
fact: Opening is the -ing form of open: making something open.
exercise: «A hook is an opening that grabs the reader, such as a question or a sudden action.» In this sentence, what does opening mean? | *The start of a story | Opening a tin of soup | A free job | A hole in a wall
exercise: «The shop's opening hours are nine till five.» What do opening hours mean? | The hours before the shop is built | Hours spent cleaning the shop | The time it takes to close the door | *The times when the shop is open
exercise: A sign says «Job opening: cook wanted.» What does opening mean here? | Opening a door for the cook | A gap in the kitchen wall | The first scene of a play | *A chance or a free job
test: «Most stories climb a story mountain: an opening, a build-up, a problem, a resolution and an ending.» In this sentence, what is an opening? | The top of a mountain | A type of boat | A gap in the rocks | *The first part of a story
test: Which word is the -ing form of open? | opened | opens | *opening | open
exam: «Setting openings can build mood, but a long list of things slows a story down.» Which meaning of opening fits best here? | *The start of a story | Opening a bottle of juice | A free job | A hole in a wall
</br>
